1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,532
That's a CTO's question
about a maintenance release.

2
00:00:07,572 --> 00:00:10,959
Someone on the team says "I
cherry-picked it, it's there."

3
00:00:10,999 --> 00:00:15,627
Someone else runs git branch --contains
with the ID of the fix, and the release

4
00:00:15,667 --> 00:00:17,163
branch is not in the list.

5
00:00:17,503 --> 00:00:22,747
Both statements can be true at the same
time. To see how, you need to know one thing

6
00:00:22,787 --> 00:00:27,590
about cherry-pick that the name hides:
nothing is picked up and carried over.

7
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:30,861
A change is recomputed, and
a new commit is written.

8
00:00:30,901 --> 00:00:34,887
Git keeps no link between the two.
Keep both statements in mind.

9
00:00:34,927 --> 00:00:35,830
They come back.

10
00:00:37,670 --> 00:00:41,394
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:41,434 --> 00:00:46,391
This video starts the module on cherry-pick.
You have, in fact, been using it for

12
00:00:46,431 --> 00:00:49,723
eight videos: every step of
a rebase is a cherry-pick.

13
00:00:49,763 --> 00:00:52,376
Today you look at the
operation on its own.

14
00:00:52,716 --> 00:00:57,419
The textbook's statement of the central
fact: a cherry-pick does not move or reuse a

15
00:00:57,459 --> 00:01:02,162
commit. It recomputes the change that a
commit made and records that change as a new

16
00:01:02,202 --> 00:01:06,623
commit, with a new ID, on the branch you
are on. Git stores no link between the

17
00:01:06,663 --> 00:01:11,367
original and the copy. A commit, remember,
is one saved snapshot of the project, and

18
00:01:11,407 --> 00:01:13,908
a branch is a name that
points at one commit.

19
00:01:14,248 --> 00:01:18,674
So there are two things to learn. How
the change is recomputed: as a three-way

20
00:01:18,714 --> 00:01:23,427
merge, which combines two versions against
a common base, and with a base you might

21
00:01:23,467 --> 00:01:28,065
not expect. And what the new commit shares
with the original: less than you would

22
00:01:28,105 --> 00:01:28,408
think.

23
00:01:28,748 --> 00:01:32,570
Then the options. -x writes the
missing link into the message.

24
00:01:32,610 --> 00:01:36,682
Then -e, -n, and -m, without which
a merge commit can't be picked.

25
00:01:36,722 --> 00:01:40,917
And last, the order in which several
commits or a range are applied.

26
00:01:41,257 --> 00:01:46,196
The project is gateway, a small service
that forwards prompts to a model API.

27
00:01:46,236 --> 00:01:51,627
main is the development line, and release/1.4
is the maintenance line that customers

28
00:01:51,667 --> 00:01:51,886
run.

29
00:01:53,726 --> 00:01:55,304
Here's what you'll walk away with.

30
00:01:55,644 --> 00:01:57,056
After this video you can:

31
00:01:57,396 --> 00:02:00,071
Name the base, ours and
theirs of a cherry-pick.

32
00:02:00,411 --> 00:02:04,997
Explain why the copy has a new ID, and
which fields it shares with the original.

33
00:02:05,337 --> 00:02:10,090
Use -x, -e, -n and, for
a merge commit, -m.

34
00:02:10,430 --> 00:02:14,239
Pick several commits and a range, and
state the order in which they are applied.

35
00:02:14,579 --> 00:02:17,677
Explain why "cherry-pick
applies a patch" is imprecise.

36
00:02:19,517 --> 00:02:23,701
In one sentence: git cherry-pick <commit>
🟡 CAUTION takes the difference between a

37
00:02:23,741 --> 00:02:27,667
commit and its parent, merges that
difference into your current branch with a

38
00:02:27,707 --> 00:02:29,727
three-way merge, and commits the result.

39
00:02:30,067 --> 00:02:35,840
Precisely. A cherry-pick is a three-way
merge with an unusual choice of base.

40
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:41,880
Call the picked commit C and its parent
P. On screen, C is f98ffd3 on main, P is

41
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:47,089
ca6dd48, one step before it, and
HEAD, where you are, is release/1.4.

42
00:02:47,429 --> 00:02:52,525
The merge computes "what changed from P to
C" and "what changed from P to HEAD" and

43
00:02:52,565 --> 00:02:56,671
combines the two. Whatever else
exists on C's branch plays no part.

44
00:02:57,011 --> 00:03:01,768
Compare with the revert from the undo module,
the commit that undoes another commit.

45
00:03:01,808 --> 00:03:05,365
There the base was the commit
itself and theirs was its parent.

46
00:03:05,405 --> 00:03:09,705
A cherry-pick is the mirror image: base
is the parent, theirs is the commit.

47
00:03:09,745 --> 00:03:11,475
Same machinery, inputs swapped.

48
00:03:11,815 --> 00:03:16,518
Why is "it applies a patch" imprecise? A
patch is a change written out as a diff.

49
00:03:16,558 --> 00:03:20,382
It carries a few lines of context
and fails when they don't match.

50
00:03:20,422 --> 00:03:23,778
The merge works with three
complete versions of each file.

51
00:03:23,818 --> 00:03:28,287
You'll see a change that is refused as
a patch and succeeds as a cherry-pick.

52
00:03:28,627 --> 00:03:33,592
Inside .git: one new commit object, with
new tree and blob objects where content is

53
00:03:33,632 --> 00:03:38,536
new. The current branch ref advances by
one commit, and the HEAD reflog, the local

54
00:03:38,576 --> 00:03:42,938
list of where HEAD has been, gets an
entry that starts with cherry-pick:.

55
00:03:42,978 --> 00:03:47,762
ORIG_HEAD is not written. While a pick is
stopped, the root ref CHERRY_PICK_HEAD

56
00:03:47,802 --> 00:03:50,958
names the commit being picked.
That's the next video.

57
00:03:51,298 --> 00:03:55,833
The new commit. It shares the author,
the author date and the message with the

58
00:03:55,873 --> 00:04:00,525
original, and nothing else. A different
tree, a different parent and a different

59
00:04:00,565 --> 00:04:05,276
committer line, the line for whoever created
this commit object, give a different

60
00:04:05,316 --> 00:04:08,561
hash. And no field points from
the copy to the original.

61
00:04:08,901 --> 00:04:09,635
The options.

62
00:04:09,975 --> 00:04:14,849
-x records where the change came from:
it appends the line "(cherry picked from

63
00:04:14,889 --> 00:04:19,887
commit", the full ID, to the message. That
line is the only link between copy and

64
00:04:19,927 --> 00:04:24,862
original that travels with the history.
The manual's advice is to use it between

65
00:04:24,902 --> 00:04:30,025
publicly visible branches and not for commits
from a private branch, whose IDs mean

66
00:04:30,065 --> 00:04:31,455
nothing to anyone else.

67
00:04:31,795 --> 00:04:34,042
-e opens the editor on the message.

68
00:04:34,382 --> 00:04:36,613
-n 🟡 applies the change
without committing.

69
00:04:36,953 --> 00:04:41,331
-m <parent-number> is required for a merge
commit, because "the difference between a

70
00:04:41,371 --> 00:04:44,645
commit and its parent" is ambiguous
when there are two parents.

71
00:04:44,985 --> 00:04:50,331
Others in brief, from the textbook: -s
adds a Signed-off-by trailer, -S signs the

72
00:04:50,371 --> 00:04:55,517
new commit, -X passes an option to the
merge machinery, and --ff fast-forwards

73
00:04:55,557 --> 00:05:00,305
instead of copying when HEAD already
is the parent of the picked commit.

74
00:05:00,645 --> 00:05:06,122
Several commits and ranges. Given several
commits, cherry-pick copies them one after

75
00:05:06,162 --> 00:05:11,574
the other. Given a range, it copies the
commits of the range from oldest to newest.

76
00:05:11,614 --> 00:05:15,581
Commits that you list are applied
in the order you list them.

77
00:05:15,621 --> 00:05:20,967
A range A..B means "reachable from B and
not from A", so A itself is not included.

78
00:05:21,007 --> 00:05:22,675
Write A^..B to include it.

79
00:05:23,015 --> 00:05:27,541
When not to cherry-pick? When a merge would
do. A pick creates a second commit for

80
00:05:27,581 --> 00:05:31,494
the same change, and the module's
third video is about what that costs.

81
00:05:33,334 --> 00:05:37,836
The textbook's analogy: a colleague edited
another copy of a document with change

82
00:05:37,876 --> 00:05:40,976
tracking on. You don't paste
their paragraph over yours.

83
00:05:41,016 --> 00:05:45,350
You ask what they changed, compared with
what they started from, and make that

84
00:05:45,390 --> 00:05:48,434
change in your copy, which
has drifted in the meantime.

85
00:05:48,774 --> 00:05:53,744
It breaks at judgment. A person notices
when the change refers to a definition that

86
00:05:53,784 --> 00:05:58,693
exists only in the other copy. Git does
not: the change applies, and your document

87
00:05:58,733 --> 00:06:03,281
is now wrong. That's the second of the
CTO's questions in this chapter, "the

88
00:06:03,321 --> 00:06:07,627
backport applied without a single
conflict, and the build broke anyway".

89
00:06:07,667 --> 00:06:12,154
A backport is a fix copied from the
development line to a maintenance line.

90
00:06:12,194 --> 00:06:16,561
And this is the semantic conflict from
the merge module in a new costume.

91
00:06:16,901 --> 00:06:21,164
The textbook also gives you a rule for
which model of a commit to use when.

92
00:06:21,204 --> 00:06:25,696
"A commit is a diff" is the right model
for predicting what a cherry-pick does.

93
00:06:25,736 --> 00:06:30,401
"A commit is a snapshot with parents" is
the right model for understanding what it

94
00:06:30,441 --> 00:06:35,163
leaves behind. On screen is what it leaves
behind: two commits, and no line between

95
00:06:35,203 --> 00:06:35,450
them.

96
00:06:37,290 --> 00:06:43,134
No line connects f98ffd3 and 3c81466.
That missing line is the subject of the

97
00:06:43,174 --> 00:06:44,433
opening question.

98
00:06:46,273 --> 00:06:52,158
Step 1: before. Asha fixed a crash on main.
The release branch has one commit of its

99
00:06:52,198 --> 00:06:52,440
own.

100
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:58,686
The fix is f98ffd3, by Asha
Rao. Its parent is ca6dd48.

101
00:06:58,726 --> 00:07:06,470
The release branch is at 3056255,
"Allow three retries on the 1.4 line".

102
00:07:06,810 --> 00:07:12,561
Two added lines. Look at the context of
the hunk, the block of changed lines with

103
00:07:12,601 --> 00:07:16,493
its surroundings: it includes
the line MAX_RETRIES = 2.

104
00:07:16,533 --> 00:07:21,140
On release/1.4 that line says 3.
Try the change as a plain patch.

105
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:24,405
"Patch does not apply."
The context doesn't match.

106
00:07:24,745 --> 00:07:29,309
Step 2: predict the pick. git merge-tree,
from the merge module, performs a merge

107
00:07:29,349 --> 00:07:33,514
without touching the working tree and
prints the ID of the resulting tree.

108
00:07:33,554 --> 00:07:36,470
Give it the parent of the
picked commit as the base.

109
00:07:36,810 --> 00:07:42,333
A tree ID beginning 1a4fb13. Now your
prediction. Base, ours, theirs for the

110
00:07:42,373 --> 00:07:48,336
MAX_RETRIES line: 2, 3, 2. Which row of
the three-way table is that, and what will

111
00:07:48,376 --> 00:07:51,484
the result say? Say it
out loud. I'll wait.

112
00:07:56,324 --> 00:08:01,354
"Only ours changed it": the result keeps
3. The cherry-pick succeeded where the

113
00:08:01,394 --> 00:08:06,232
patch failed, and git rev-parse HEAD^{tree}
prints the same tree ID that the

114
00:08:06,272 --> 00:08:07,900
three-way merge predicted.

115
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:13,246
The file keeps the release's MAX_RETRIES
= 3 and gains the two new lines: each

116
00:08:13,286 --> 00:08:16,157
side's change relative
to the base, combined.

117
00:08:16,497 --> 00:08:21,073
Note the line "Author: Asha Rao" in the
output. Git prints it because the author

118
00:08:21,113 --> 00:08:22,920
differs from the committer, you.

119
00:08:23,260 --> 00:08:25,289
Step 3: the two objects.

120
00:08:32,629 --> 00:08:37,275
Read the two objects once more and look for
a field that points from the copy to the

121
00:08:37,315 --> 00:08:38,614
original. There is none.

122
00:08:38,954 --> 00:08:41,438
The reflog entry starts with cherry-pick:.

123
00:08:41,778 --> 00:08:46,777
And in the graph: two commits with the
same author and subject, on two branches,

124
00:08:46,817 --> 00:08:50,304
unrelated. So both statements
from the opening are true.

125
00:08:50,344 --> 00:08:53,201
The fix is there, and
--contains can't see it.

126
00:08:53,541 --> 00:08:58,459
Step 4: what it demands of your working
tree. labs/run ch10/pick-dirty.

127
00:08:58,499 --> 00:09:04,256
The manual says the working tree must be
clean. Git 2.55 is more precise than that.

128
00:09:04,296 --> 00:09:08,655
Three cases. An unstaged edit in
a file the pick doesn't touch.

129
00:09:08,695 --> 00:09:14,382
An unstaged edit in the file it changes.
And a staged change in an untouched file.

130
00:09:14,422 --> 00:09:20,108
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: for
each, write "goes through" or "refused".

131
00:09:22,448 --> 00:09:26,999
Case one goes through: an unstaged edit
in a file the pick does not write is left

132
00:09:27,039 --> 00:09:27,339
alone.

133
00:09:27,679 --> 00:09:32,372
Case two is refused: an unstaged edit in
a file it must write stops the command.

134
00:09:32,712 --> 00:09:37,826
Case three is refused too: any staged
change stops it, even in a file the pick

135
00:09:37,866 --> 00:09:38,751
doesn't touch.

136
00:09:39,091 --> 00:09:43,845
Most people miss that one. In both
refusals the command stops before it does

137
00:09:43,885 --> 00:09:45,737
anything, and nothing is lost.

138
00:09:46,077 --> 00:09:52,397
Step 5: -e and -x. labs/run ch10/pick-options.
In this replay a scripted editor

139
00:09:52,437 --> 00:09:56,826
shows the message as Git
opened it and as it was saved.

140
00:10:00,166 --> 00:10:03,576
With -x, the line "(cherry
picked from commit f98ffd3..."

141
00:10:03,616 --> 00:10:08,237
is already in the buffer when the editor
opens. The scripted editor added one

142
00:10:08,277 --> 00:10:12,596
paragraph, the reason for the backport,
and kept the rest, as you would.

143
00:10:12,636 --> 00:10:15,985
Delete the last line in the
editor and the link is gone.

144
00:10:16,325 --> 00:10:22,167
Step 6: -n. Quick quiz, two options. Who
is the author of the resulting commit?

145
00:10:22,207 --> 00:10:26,782
Option one: Asha, as before.
Option two: you. Say it out loud.

146
00:10:31,622 --> 00:10:36,824
The change is staged, and CHERRY_PICK_HEAD
does not exist: as far as Git's state is

147
00:10:36,864 --> 00:10:39,413
concerned, no cherry-pick is in progress.

148
00:10:39,753 --> 00:10:45,131
Option two. Author: Lab User. Not Asha.
The original message is still on offer,

149
00:10:45,171 --> 00:10:50,617
because Git leaves it in .git/MERGE_MSG
and a git commit without -m proposes it.

150
00:10:50,657 --> 00:10:56,377
The authorship is not. Use -n to combine
several picks into one commit or to adapt a

151
00:10:56,417 --> 00:11:01,521
change before committing it, and restore
the credit yourself: git commit -C

152
00:11:01,561 --> 00:11:05,361
<original> reuses the original's
message and authorship.

153
00:11:05,701 --> 00:11:10,197
Step 7: a merge commit.
labs/run ch10/pick-merge.

154
00:11:15,737 --> 00:11:20,526
"Is a merge but no -m option was given."
The same refusal as for reverting a merge,

155
00:11:20,566 --> 00:11:21,690
for the same reason.

156
00:11:22,030 --> 00:11:27,276
-m 1 says: take parent 1 as the base. The
change relative to the first parent is

157
00:11:27,316 --> 00:11:32,827
everything the merged branch brought in, so
the two commits of feat/limits arrive as

158
00:11:32,867 --> 00:11:33,091
one.

159
00:11:33,431 --> 00:11:38,503
Now look at the graph. The new commit is
titled "Merge branch 'feat/limits'" and has

160
00:11:38,543 --> 00:11:43,311
a single parent. It's not a merge. For
Git the commits of feat/limits are still

161
00:11:43,351 --> 00:11:48,423
unmerged here: git branch --no-merged lists
the branch, and a real merge later would

162
00:11:48,463 --> 00:11:51,526
add those commits to the
history next to this copy.

163
00:11:53,366 --> 00:11:55,014
Five mistakes to watch for.

164
00:11:55,354 --> 00:11:59,185
Proving a backport with git
branch --contains <original ID>.

165
00:11:59,225 --> 00:12:04,281
Root cause: the copy is a new commit with
a new ID and no link to the original;

166
00:12:04,321 --> 00:12:06,346
ancestry knows nothing about it.

167
00:12:06,686 --> 00:12:11,397
Picking without -x between public branches.
Root cause: no field of the commit

168
00:12:11,437 --> 00:12:16,271
object points to the original, so the
message line is the only link that travels

169
00:12:16,311 --> 00:12:17,307
with the history.

170
00:12:17,647 --> 00:12:22,944
Using -n and committing with -m. Root cause:
with -n no cherry-pick is in progress,

171
00:12:22,984 --> 00:12:28,024
so the commit gets you as author; git
commit -C <original> restores message and

172
00:12:28,064 --> 00:12:28,731
authorship.

173
00:12:29,071 --> 00:12:32,452
Believing that -m 1 on a merge
commit merges the branch.

174
00:12:32,492 --> 00:12:37,399
Root cause: the result has one parent, so
the branch's commits are still unmerged

175
00:12:37,439 --> 00:12:40,453
and a later real merge adds
them next to the copy.

176
00:12:40,793 --> 00:12:47,138
Expecting A..B to include A. Root cause:
the range is "reachable from B and not from

177
00:12:47,178 --> 00:12:48,810
A"; A^..B includes it.

178
00:12:50,650 --> 00:12:55,593
Now, out of the lab. The standard case,
from the textbook: a fix that has to exist

179
00:12:55,633 --> 00:13:00,212
on more than one line of development,
on main and on the release branch that

180
00:13:00,252 --> 00:13:03,980
customers run. An inference
gateway is exactly such a product.

181
00:13:04,020 --> 00:13:07,809
Customers pin a version, and main
has moved on to the next API.

182
00:13:08,149 --> 00:13:12,178
The second everyday case is a commit made
on the wrong branch: pick it onto the

183
00:13:12,218 --> 00:13:16,298
right one, then remove it from the wrong
one, with the tools of the undo module.

184
00:13:16,638 --> 00:13:21,316
And the warning that goes with both. A
clean cherry-pick is a new snapshot that

185
00:13:21,356 --> 00:13:26,155
nobody has run. The fix on main may call
a helper that was introduced two commits

186
00:13:26,195 --> 00:13:29,440
earlier on main and does not
exist on the release line.

187
00:13:29,480 --> 00:13:34,398
The pick applies. The release is broken.
Git has compared text in three versions of

188
00:13:34,438 --> 00:13:39,415
the files the commit touched, and nothing
else. Run the release branch's tests after

189
00:13:39,455 --> 00:13:41,864
every pick, as you would after any merge.

190
00:13:43,704 --> 00:13:50,708
Your turn. Do Exercise 10.1, Level 1, "one
commit, copied to another branch", in

191
00:13:50,748 --> 00:13:53,613
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

192
00:13:53,953 --> 00:13:55,692
Predict in writing before the pick:

193
00:13:56,032 --> 00:14:00,111
The three inputs: which commit is the
base, which is ours, which is theirs.

194
00:14:00,451 --> 00:14:05,599
For a line that differs between the two
branches near the change: its value in base,

195
00:14:05,639 --> 00:14:07,760
ours and theirs, and in the result.

196
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:12,039
Of the five fields of the new commit
object, which will equal the original's?

197
00:14:12,379 --> 00:14:16,888
After the pick, check the third prediction
with git cat-file -p on both commits.

198
00:14:17,228 --> 00:14:22,756
The challenge is Exercise 10.8, Level 3,
"a clean pick that does not work", in the

199
00:14:22,796 --> 00:14:23,435
same file.

200
00:14:25,275 --> 00:14:28,957
Q144: "Why does cherry-pick
create a new commit?"

201
00:14:31,297 --> 00:14:35,924
Answer out loud first. It's a short
question, and a strong answer is not short.

202
00:14:35,964 --> 00:14:40,886
It starts from what a commit ID is computed
from and goes through the fields, saying

203
00:14:40,926 --> 00:14:45,789
which are bound to differ on another branch.
It explains how the content of the new

204
00:14:45,829 --> 00:14:48,625
commit is computed, with
the three inputs named.

205
00:14:48,965 --> 00:14:53,187
And it draws the consequence that matters
in practice: what Git can and cannot tell

206
00:14:53,227 --> 00:14:57,397
you afterwards about the relationship
between the two commits, and what you can do

207
00:14:57,437 --> 00:14:59,605
at the moment of the
pick to leave a trace.

208
00:15:01,445 --> 00:15:05,573
Let's land this. You should now be
able to say, in your own words:

209
00:15:05,913 --> 00:15:10,764
A cherry-pick is a three-way merge with the
picked commit's parent as base, HEAD as

210
00:15:10,804 --> 00:15:15,360
ours and the picked commit as theirs. It
works on whole files, which is why it

211
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:20,134
succeeds where a patch's context would not
match. The result is a new commit that

212
00:15:20,174 --> 00:15:25,025
keeps the author, author date and message
and has a new tree, parent and committer,

213
00:15:25,065 --> 00:15:28,266
so its ID is new and no field
links it to the original.

214
00:15:28,606 --> 00:15:33,353
-x writes that link into the message. -n
stages without committing and loses the

215
00:15:33,393 --> 00:15:38,378
authorship. -m chooses the base parent for
a merge commit, and the result is still a

216
00:15:38,418 --> 00:15:43,164
single-parent commit. A range is applied
oldest first and excludes its left end.

217
00:15:45,004 --> 00:15:48,588
Read sections 10.1 to 10.5 of Chapter 10.

218
00:15:48,928 --> 00:15:55,802
Do Exercise 10.3, Level 1, "two commits
picked as one", and Exercise 10.5, Level 2,

219
00:15:55,842 --> 00:16:02,216
"which commits does a range pick?", both
in exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

220
00:16:02,556 --> 00:16:07,729
Today you took cherry-pick apart: three
inputs, one new commit, and no link unless

221
00:16:07,769 --> 00:16:12,942
you write one. Do Exercise 10.1 before the
next video, and write your three inputs

222
00:16:12,982 --> 00:16:18,091
down first. Next time: CHERRY_PICK_HEAD,
the sequencer, conflicts, and picks that

223
00:16:18,131 --> 00:16:22,605
are already there. Until then, look
at the state first and type second.

224
00:16:22,645 --> 00:16:24,130
See you in the next one.
